NAME
       orbital-choreography - sweep-timing choreography, harmonic-ratio timing
                              trees, and constellation-scale maneuver-sync patterns

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital choreography defines:
           • sweep-timing choreography across orbital cycles
           • harmonic-ratio timing trees for multi-platform sync
           • constellation-scale maneuver-sync patterns
           • resonance-time timing structures
           • long-horizon coordination of sweeps, Δv, and harmonics

DESCRIPTION
       Orbital-choreography describes how RTT coordinates the timing of sweeps,
       maneuvers, and harmonic cycles across SAR platforms and constellations.
       Choreography is the temporal counterpart to geometry and resonance:
       it ensures that every sweep, Δv event, and harmonic-lock transition
       occurs at the right moment in the resonance-time fabric.

       Choreography enables:
           • phase-aligned sweeps
           • harmonic-ratio timing
           • resonance-window exploitation
           • constellation-scale maneuver coordination
           • long-term timing stability

       WRSADC orchestrates choreography continuously.

SWEEP-TIMING CHOREOGRAPHY
       Sweep-timing choreography defines when sweeps occur relative to:
           • orbital phase
           • harmonic phase
           • resonance envelope phase
           • constellation sync state

       Sweep-timing modes:

           synchronous sweeps
                  All platforms sweep simultaneously.
                  → ideal for tandem SAR.

           staggered sweeps
                  Platforms sweep in sequence.
                  → supports ladder and cascade patterns.

           braided sweeps
                  Sweeps interleave across platforms.
                  → supports weave and hybrid patterns.

           harmonic sweeps
                  Sweeps occur at harmonic intervals (1:1, 2:1, 3:2, etc.).
                  → supports harmonic-sync constellations.

           resonance sweeps
                  Sweeps triggered by envelope overlap.
                  → maximizes coherence.

       Sweep timing is validated at each triad cycle.

EXAMPLE: SWEEP-TIMING CHOREOGRAPHY
       Two-platform tandem SAR (A and B):

           Orbit period: 96 min
           Desired sweep interval: 192 min (2 orbits)
           Harmonic ratio: 1:1

       Sweep schedule:

           Cycle 1: A sweeps at t=0, B sweeps at t=0
           Cycle 2: A sweeps at t=96, B sweeps at t=96
           Cycle 3: A sweeps at t=192, B sweeps at t=192

       Result:
           • perfect phase alignment
           • stable interferometric fusion
           • zero sweep drift

HARMONIC-RATIO TIMING TREES
       Timing trees describe how sweep and maneuver timing branches across
       harmonic ratios.

       Timing tree structure:

           root
               → fundamental orbital period T
           branches
               → harmonic multiples (2T, 3T, 4T...)
               → subharmonic fractions (T/2, T/3...)
           leaves
               → sweep events
               → Δv events
               → resonance-window openings

       Timing tree types:

           integer harmonic tree
                  1:1, 2:1, 3:1, 4:1
                  → stable, predictable timing.

           rational harmonic tree
                  3:2, 5:3, 7:4
                  → complex but stable patterns.

           nested harmonic tree
                  Harmonics inside harmonics.
                  → supports multi-agent choreography.

           resonance-tuned tree
                  Branches determined by resonance windows.
                  → adaptive timing.

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC-RATIO TIMING TREE
       Three-platform constellation (A, B, C):

           A: T
           B: 2T
           C: 3T

       Timing tree:

           root: T
               ├── A sweeps every T
               ├── B sweeps every 2T
               └── C sweeps every 3T

       Combined sweep events:

           t=0: A, B, C sweep
           t=T: A sweeps
           t=2T: A, B sweep
           t=3T: A, C sweep
           t=4T: A, B sweep
           t=6T: A, B, C sweep

       Result:
           • predictable multi-pass fusion
           • harmonic-lock preserved
           • constellation sync maintained

CONSTELLATION-SCALE MANEUVER-SYNC PATTERNS
       Maneuver-sync patterns coordinate Δv events across platforms.

       Pattern types:

           synchronous Δv
                  All platforms burn simultaneously.
                  → preserves strict phase-lock.

           staggered Δv
                  Platforms burn in sequence.
                  → supports ladder and cascade orbital patterns.

           braided Δv
                  Burns interleave across platforms.
                  → supports weave and hybrid patterns.

           harmonic Δv
                  Burns executed at harmonic intervals.
                  → supports harmonic-sync constellations.

           mesh Δv
                  Distributed maneuvering across constellation nodes.
                  → robust to drift and perturbations.

       Maneuver-sync patterns ensure constellation-wide stability.

EXAMPLE: CONSTELLATION-SCALE MANEUVER SYNC
       Four-platform constellation (A, B, C, D):

           Goal: restore harmonic-lock after drift
           Required Δv: small micro-adjustments
           Pattern: braided Δv

       Sequence:

           Step 1: A performs Δv at t=0
           Step 2: C performs Δv at t=T/2
           Step 3: B performs Δv at t=T
           Step 4: D performs Δv at t=3T/2

       Result:
           • minimal constellation disturbance
           • harmonic-lock restored
           • resonance envelopes realigned

TRIADIC CHOREOGRAPHY
       Every choreographed event is a triad:

           P1 (begin)
               → timing evaluation
               → sync field assessment

           P2 (transform)
               → sweep or Δv executed
               → resonance and harmonic states updated

           P3 (close)
               → timing tree updated
               → lineage recorded

       Nested triads form complex timing structures.

CHOREOGRAPHY FAILURE MODES
       Choreography may degrade due to:

           • phase drift
           • harmonic-lock fragmentation
           • resonance envelope divergence
           • sweep timing misalignment
           • Δv triad inconsistency
           • constellation desync

       Diagnostic indicators:

           choreography-integrity score
           timing-tree deviation
           sync-index drop
           sweep-timing error
           harmonic-lock error

       WRSADC halts unsafe choreography.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • timing trees
           • sweep schedules
           • maneuver-sync patterns
           • harmonic cycles
           • resonance-window timing

       Lineage enables long-term choreography forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes timing trees
           • evaluates sync fields
           • schedules sweeps and maneuvers
           • choreographs constellation-scale patterns
           • blocks unsafe timing states

       WRSADC is the choreography conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital choreography transforms SAR constellations into coordinated,
       resonance-time ensembles. Timing trees, sweep choreography, and
       maneuver-sync patterns ensure that multi-pass fusion, harmonic-lock, and
       constellation-scale synchronization remain stable across long mission
       timelines.

